{{nihongo|'''Kiyokazu Abo'''|安保 清種|Abo Kiyokazu}} (14 November 1893 – 22 August 1976) was a Sanese politician and bureaucrat who served as the 21st [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] from October 1942 to March 1943. A member of the [[Imperial Rule Assistance Association|Taisei Yokusankai]], his short-lived government struggled to manage the escalating resource shortages caused by the [[Volisania|Volisanian]] oil embargo and the strain of maintaining the nation's war footing, leading to his resignation after only five months in office. His premiership is remembered as a transitional period that exposed the structural weaknesses of the one-party state under wartime conditions.

== Early life ==
Abo was born in [[Toyozawa]] in 1893, the son of a mid-ranking civil servant in the Home Ministry. His family's modest bureaucratic background placed him firmly within the emerging professional class that would come to dominate the Imperial civil service. He attended the prestigious [[Imperial University of Okami]], graduating with a degree in law in 1916.

Upon graduation, Abo entered the Ministry of Commerce and Industry, where he distinguished himself as a competent administrator with a particular talent for industrial logistics. During the 1920s, he rose steadily through the bureaucratic ranks, earning a reputation as a pragmatic technocrat rather than an ideologue. His early career coincided with the final years of the [[Go-Shōkō Democracy]] period, and he was known within the ministry for his skepticism toward the growing influence of military factions in economic planning.

== Political career ==
Abo's political transformation came with the dissolution of political parties into the [[Imperial Rule Assistance Association|Taisei Yokusankai]] in 1940. Like many career bureaucrats, he joined the new organization not out of ideological conviction but because it offered the only viable path to continued influence in government. His administrative experience and reputation for competence made him a valuable asset to the ruling establishment.

He served as Vice-Minister of Commerce and Industry under Prime Minister [[Fumio Gotō]], where he was responsible for implementing the state control over industry and the press that characterized the early Taisei Yokusankai period. His work on the National Mobilization Law's economic provisions gave him intimate knowledge of the nation's industrial capacity—and its vulnerabilities.

When Gotō's government faltered under the weight of the Volisanian oil embargo and the growing strain on the domestic economy, Abo was tapped as his successor. His selection reflected the Taisei Yokusankai's preference for bureaucratic administrators over charismatic leaders during periods of crisis.

== Premiership (1942–1943) ==
[[File:Volisanian Oil Embargo Impact 1943.png|thumb|right|200px|The impact of the Volisanian oil embargo on the Sanese wartime economy]]
Abo took office on 8 October 1942, inheriting a government already buckling under the pressures of the wartime economy. The [[Volisania|Volisanian]] oil embargo, which had been imposed in response to the Mitoyagi Empire's alignment with the [[Coastal Powers]], had created severe shortages in petroleum, rubber, and other critical materials. The nation's strategic reserves were depleting faster than anticipated, and the domestic rationing system was proving inadequate to the task.

Abo's primary challenge was managing the resource crisis while maintaining the nation's war footing. He attempted to accelerate the development of synthetic fuel production and expand the recycling of strategic materials, but these programs were hampered by chronic shortages of industrial equipment and skilled labor. The military, which consumed an ever-increasing share of the national budget, resisted any reduction in its allocation of resources.

Internationally, Abo's government sought to strengthen the [[Mitoyagi-Zong Treaty|Mitoyagi-Zong alliance]] to secure alternative supply routes through the Shendan. However, the disruption of Shendan trade routes by Illypnian naval operations made this increasingly difficult. Abo's attempts to negotiate a limited resumption of trade with neutral powers were rebuffed, as the Illypnian coalition tightened its economic encirclement.

By early 1943, the situation had become untenable. Food riots in several major cities, a wave of industrial strikes in the steel sector, and open criticism from military leaders who accused Abo of "defeatist economics" eroded what little political capital he possessed. On 17 March 1943, Abo tendered his resignation to the Emperor, citing his inability to resolve the resource crisis. He was succeeded by [[Reijirō Wakatsuki]], a former democratic leader brought back to lead the Taisei Yokusankai government as a moderate, unifying figure.

=== Domestic policy ===
The Abo government's domestic policy was dominated by the resource crisis and its consequences for the civilian population. The government introduced the Emergency Rationing Ordinance of November 1942, which established a comprehensive rationing system for food, fuel, and clothing. The ordinance allocated scarce resources according to a priority system that placed military needs first, essential industries second, and civilian consumption last. The rationing system, while necessary, was deeply unpopular, particularly in the urban areas where food shortages were most acute.

The government also attempted to address the labor crisis through the National Service Expansion Act of December 1942, which extended labor conscription to women and older workers and increased the maximum hours that could be required of industrial workers. The Act was a significant expansion of the state's authority over the civilian population, and it was implemented through the Taisei Yokusankai's neighborhood associations, which were responsible for identifying and mobilizing workers.

On the industrial front, Abo's government attempted to accelerate the development of synthetic fuel production. The Synthetic Fuel Development Act of January 1943 authorized significant investment in coal liquefaction and shale oil extraction, but the programs were hampered by chronic shortages of industrial equipment and skilled labor. The synthetic fuel program would never produce more than a fraction of the nation's petroleum needs, but it represented the government's recognition that the Volisanian embargo was a long-term strategic challenge rather than a temporary inconvenience.

=== Foreign policy ===
Beyond the Mitoyagi-Zong alliance, the Abo government pursued several foreign policy initiatives. The government attempted to negotiate a limited resumption of trade with neutral powers, particularly Moselle, which had maintained a limited humanitarian corridor through the Illypnian naval blockade. The Mosellan Sanitary Corridor Agreement of December 1942, which allowed the shipment of medical supplies and food through Mosellan-controlled ports, was a modest diplomatic achievement that provided critical relief to the civilian population.

The government also sought to strengthen the relationship with [[Zong]] through the Mitoyagi-Zong Treaty framework. Abo dispatched a high-level diplomatic mission to Zong in January 1943 to negotiate increased resource shipments and military cooperation, but the mission was only partially successful. The disruption of Shendan supply routes by Illypnian naval operations made it increasingly difficult to transport Zong resources to the home islands, and the Zong government was itself under growing pressure from the Illypnian coalition.

The Abo government also confronted the growing problem of Illypnian strategic bombing. The Volisanian and Breisland air forces had begun conducting bombing raids against Sanese industrial targets, and the government's air defense capabilities were inadequate to protect the civilian population. The Civil Air Defense Act of February 1943 expanded the air raid precaution system and authorized the construction of additional bomb shelters, but the resources available for civil defense were limited by the competing demands of military production.

== Legacy ==
Abo's premiership is generally regarded by historians as a failure of bureaucratic governance under wartime conditions. His technocratic approach, which had served him well in peacetime administration, proved inadequate to the scale of the crisis facing the nation. The resource shortages that defined his tenure would continue to worsen under subsequent administrations, ultimately contributing to the devastating conditions that led to the [[Armistice of Okami]] in 1952.

However, some scholars have argued that Abo's assessment of the resource situation was more accurate than that of his military critics, and that his resignation marked a missed opportunity to pursue a negotiated settlement before the conflict escalated further. His post-war writings, published in 1968 under the title ''Crisis and Compromise'', provided valuable insights into the internal deliberations of the Taisei Yokusankai government during the critical period of 1942–1943.

After his resignation, Abo retired from public life. He lived quietly in [[Okami]] until his death in 1976 at the age of 82.

